{% set local_problem %}
<p>Decatur homeowners face gutter challenges most cities don't. The Tennessee River wraps around your city. Wheeler Lake sits east. The Wheeler Wildlife Refuge covers thousands of acres of wetland to the north. Water is everywhere — including under your house.</p>

<p>Decatur's water table sits higher than most of North Alabama. During wet seasons, groundwater can rise to within 3-4 feet of foundations. When gutters overflow or fail, you're adding roof runoff to soil already saturated from below. That's hydrostatic pressure against your basement walls. That's moisture creeping into places it shouldn't.</p>

<p>The city's housing mix makes this complicated. <a href="/recent-projects/">Historic homes in Albany and downtown</a> were built on higher ground — those early settlers knew what they were doing — but many have undersized original gutters. Meanwhile, newer construction in <a href="/recent-projects/">Southwest Decatur</a> and along Highway 31 came with builder-grade systems: 5-inch gutters, .027 gauge aluminum, hangers at 32-inch spacing. Technically code-compliant. Practically inadequate for river-town rainfall.</p>

<p>Then there's humidity. Sectional gutters rely on sealant at every joint. In Decatur's muggy river air, that sealant degrades faster than anywhere else we serve. We regularly see 8-10 year old sectional gutters leaking at every seam. Seamless gutters eliminate 80%+ of those joints. Fewer weak points means longer life.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> If you're in Decatur and your gutters are over 10 years old with visible seam issues, replace the whole system. Patching seams in this humidity is temporary at best — maybe 2 years before they fail again. The math doesn't work out.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Seamless gutters are exactly what they sound like: continuous gutters with no seams except at corners and downspout connections. Unlike sectional gutters made of 10-foot sections joined together, seamless gutters are fabricated on-site from one continuous roll of aluminum coil — cut to your home's exact run lengths.</p>

<p><strong>What we install in Decatur:</strong> 6-inch K-style gutters in .032 gauge aluminum. Hidden hangers at 24-inch intervals (tighter than the 32-inch code minimum). 3x4 downspouts positioned every 35-40 linear feet. Everything measured and fabricated in your driveway.</p>

<p><strong>Why these specs matter here:</strong> 6-inch gutters hold about 40% more water than 5-inch. That matters when 2 inches falls in an hour during spring storms. The .032 gauge is about 20% thicker than builder-grade .027 — it resists denting and holds shape better over 20+ years. And 24-inch hanger spacing prevents the sagging we see on older systems with wider spacing.</p>

<p>For <a href="/recent-projects/">historic homes in Albany</a>, we also offer half-round profiles in copper or painted aluminum. Period-appropriate aesthetics with modern performance — the best of both worlds.</p>

<p><strong>The honest bit:</strong> "Seamless" is slightly misleading. You still have seams at corners and downspout drops. What you eliminate is the seam every 10 feet in the middle of runs. Those mid-run seams fail first — they expand and contract differently than the gutter body, and river-town humidity eats the sealant alive.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've served Morgan County for over 20 years — long enough to understand what the Tennessee River does to homes. We're not a franchise following a national playbook. We're local contractors who know Decatur's specific challenges.</p>

<p>We know the difference between installing on a 1925 Albany home and a 2020 Southwest Decatur build. We know which neighborhoods have severe water table issues and which need extra-capacity downspouts. And we know that Decatur's humidity requires products and techniques that wouldn't be necessary 20 miles inland.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> We see a lot of DIY seam repair in Decatur — homeowners trying to patch leaks with caulk or silicon. It doesn't work here. Silicon isn't a gutter sealer, and in this humidity, even proper gutter sealant only buys you a year or two. If your sectional gutters are leaking at seams, replacement is usually the smarter investment.</p>

<p><strong>See our work:</strong> Check out our <a href="/recent-projects/">recent projects page</a> to see gutter installations we've done across Decatur neighborhoods.</p>
{% endset %}

